1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:02,300
Orcas hunt great white sharks.

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:05,740
They hunt blue whales,
the biggest animals

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:07,260
that have ever lived.

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:11,540
And yet, as far as anyone
knows, a wild orca has

5
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:12,620
never killed a person.

6
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:14,960
Not one confirmed case.

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:19,880
People swim, surf, kayak
and dive near them all

8
00:00:19,880 --> 00:00:20,680
over the world.

9
00:00:21,320 --> 00:00:24,060
So why do the ocean's top
predators leave us alone?

10
00:00:24,800 --> 00:00:27,500
The honest answer is that
scientists aren't completely

11
00:00:27,500 --> 00:00:27,900
sure.

12
00:00:27,900 --> 00:00:30,020
But the clues are fascinating.

13
00:00:31,880 --> 00:00:33,820
First, the name is
a bit misleading.

14
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:37,060
A killer whale is actually
a dolphin, the biggest

15
00:00:37,060 --> 00:00:38,080
dolphin there is.

16
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:41,420
A big male can grow to
about nine metres long

17
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:44,480
and weigh around six tonnes,
as much as an elephant.

18
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,800
Females can live for
eighty years or more.

19
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:51,320
Orcas live in every
ocean, from warm seas to

20
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:54,180
the ice around Antarctica,
in tight family groups

21
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:57,680
called pods, often led by an
old mother, a grandmother.

22
00:00:59,028 --> 00:01:00,848
And they are very,
very good hunters.

23
00:01:01,728 --> 00:01:04,768
Off the south coast of
Western Australia, researchers

24
00:01:04,768 --> 00:01:08,248
watched pods of orcas kill
blue whales three times,

25
00:01:08,508 --> 00:01:09,788
including a healthy adult.

26
00:01:10,608 --> 00:01:13,768
The orcas took turns tiring
the whale out, and some

27
00:01:13,768 --> 00:01:16,468
pressed down on it, so it
couldn't get up to breathe.

28
00:01:17,328 --> 00:01:20,048
With great white sharks,
orcas seem to want one

29
00:01:20,048 --> 00:01:23,908
thing: the liver, which is
huge and full of energy-rich

30
00:01:23,908 --> 00:01:24,708
oil.

31
00:01:25,108 --> 00:01:27,608
Off South Africa, sharks
have washed up with their

32
00:01:27,608 --> 00:01:28,908
livers neatly removed.

33
00:01:29,568 --> 00:01:32,468
And when orcas show up,
great whites often leave

34
00:01:32,468 --> 00:01:34,928
the area for weeks
or even months.

35
00:01:35,808 --> 00:01:37,628
So this is not a gentle animal.

36
00:01:38,348 --> 00:01:39,768
This is the ocean's
top predator.

37
00:01:41,289 --> 00:01:42,169
So what about us?

38
00:01:42,969 --> 00:01:44,529
There's one famous close call.

39
00:01:45,129 --> 00:01:49,049
In 1972, a surfer in
California was bitten on

40
00:01:49,049 --> 00:01:52,129
the leg by an orca and needed
about a hundred stitches.

41
00:01:53,029 --> 00:01:54,949
Scientists think it
mistook him for a seal,

42
00:01:55,669 --> 00:01:56,469
and it let go.

43
00:01:57,229 --> 00:02:00,089
In recent years, a
group of orcas off Spain

44
00:02:00,089 --> 00:02:02,729
and Portugal has been
pushing sailing boats

45
00:02:02,729 --> 00:02:04,109
and breaking their rudders.

46
00:02:04,189 --> 00:02:05,509
But nobody has been killed.

47
00:02:05,989 --> 00:02:08,529
Orcas have had countless
chances, and the number

48
00:02:08,529 --> 00:02:11,009
of people they've killed
in the wild, as far as

49
00:02:11,009 --> 00:02:12,389
we know, is zero.

50
00:02:14,442 --> 00:02:17,002
Clue number one: orcas
have food traditions.

51
00:02:17,882 --> 00:02:20,282
In the North Pacific,
scientists have found different

52
00:02:20,282 --> 00:02:22,362
kinds of orcas sharing
the same waters.

53
00:02:23,282 --> 00:02:26,122
Resident orcas eat fish,
mostly Chinook salmon.

54
00:02:26,902 --> 00:02:29,642
Bigg's orcas, sometimes
called transients, hunt

55
00:02:29,642 --> 00:02:31,842
seals, sea lions and porpoises.

56
00:02:32,622 --> 00:02:35,222
Offshore orcas eat fish
too, including sharks.

57
00:02:36,122 --> 00:02:38,222
They don't mix, they don't
breed with each other,

58
00:02:38,662 --> 00:02:40,982
and they even call in
different dialects.

59
00:02:41,202 --> 00:02:42,442
And their menu is learned.

60
00:02:42,442 --> 00:02:45,942
Calves learn what to eat,
and how to catch it, from

61
00:02:45,942 --> 00:02:47,282
their mothers and their pod.

62
00:02:48,102 --> 00:02:50,802
It's so fixed that fish-eating
residents, even when

63
00:02:50,802 --> 00:02:53,742
salmon is scarce, don't
switch to the seals swimming

64
00:02:53,742 --> 00:02:54,782
right past them.

65
00:02:55,402 --> 00:02:58,042
Humans have never been on
any orca family's menu.

66
00:02:58,542 --> 00:02:59,642
So nobody teaches it.

67
00:03:00,859 --> 00:03:02,639
Clue number two: sound.

68
00:03:03,479 --> 00:03:05,979
Orcas find food by
clicking and listening to

69
00:03:05,979 --> 00:03:08,279
the echoes, which is
called echolocation.

70
00:03:09,159 --> 00:03:12,379
It's so precise that
residents may even tell kinds

71
00:03:12,379 --> 00:03:15,359
of salmon apart by the echo
from a fish's swim bladder,

72
00:03:15,819 --> 00:03:17,579
the little air bag
that helps it float.

73
00:03:18,319 --> 00:03:20,539
A person probably sounds
nothing like their usual

74
00:03:20,539 --> 00:03:21,339
food.

75
00:03:21,719 --> 00:03:24,199
We're a different shape,
we move differently,

76
00:03:24,779 --> 00:03:28,799
and divers come with wetsuits,
metal tanks and bubbles.

77
00:03:29,699 --> 00:03:33,139
So to an orca, we may not
register as food at all.

78
00:03:33,839 --> 00:03:34,939
Just as something strange.

79
00:03:36,710 --> 00:03:38,530
Clue number three: the brain.

80
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:41,930
An orca's brain weighs
around five to six kilograms,

81
00:03:42,470 --> 00:03:44,190
about four times
as much as ours.

82
00:03:45,110 --> 00:03:47,950
Brain scans show it's
deeply folded, with large

83
00:03:47,950 --> 00:03:50,830
areas thought to be linked
to emotion and social

84
00:03:50,830 --> 00:03:51,630
life.

85
00:03:51,890 --> 00:03:55,090
Orcas are curious and
cautious, and they learn from

86
00:03:55,090 --> 00:03:55,890
each other.

87
00:03:56,210 --> 00:03:58,510
When something big and
unfamiliar shows up,

88
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:01,650
the sensible choice is to
check it out, and leave

89
00:04:01,650 --> 00:04:02,450
it alone.

90
00:04:03,849 --> 00:04:05,209
But here's the strange part.

91
00:04:05,789 --> 00:04:07,409
Orcas have killed people.

92
00:04:08,669 --> 00:04:09,409
Four times.

93
00:04:09,409 --> 00:04:12,869
And every one of those
deaths happened in captivity,

94
00:04:12,869 --> 00:04:14,509
at marine parks.

95
00:04:15,349 --> 00:04:19,009
Three of them involved the
same orca, a male called

96
00:04:19,009 --> 00:04:19,809
Tilikum.

97
00:04:20,389 --> 00:04:23,929
He was captured off
Iceland in 1983 when he was

98
00:04:23,929 --> 00:04:27,389
about two years old, and
spent the rest of his life

99
00:04:27,389 --> 00:04:30,449
in tanks, with orcas
that weren't his family.

100
00:04:31,209 --> 00:04:34,869
Many scientists think life
in a tank, with no family,

101
00:04:35,189 --> 00:04:38,809
no space and nothing like
a wild orca's day, can

102
00:04:38,809 --> 00:04:40,569
lead to stress and aggression.

103
00:04:41,369 --> 00:04:43,709
So the captive record
doesn't contradict the wild

104
00:04:43,709 --> 00:04:44,509
one.

105
00:04:44,729 --> 00:04:47,629
It shows what can go wrong
when you take a clever,

106
00:04:47,629 --> 00:04:50,469
social animal away from
everything it knows.

107
00:04:52,401 --> 00:04:54,921
So why don't wild orcas kill us?

108
00:04:55,581 --> 00:04:56,621
Put the clues together.

109
00:04:57,441 --> 00:04:58,881
We're not on their learned menu.

110
00:04:59,641 --> 00:05:02,541
We probably sound
strange, not tasty.

111
00:05:03,361 --> 00:05:06,001
And they're careful animals
that don't attack things

112
00:05:06,001 --> 00:05:07,201
they don't understand.

113
00:05:08,061 --> 00:05:11,061
Even so, researchers who
spend their lives with

114
00:05:11,061 --> 00:05:13,841
orcas admit they can't
fully explain it.

115
00:05:14,381 --> 00:05:15,901
Maybe it's a mix of all three.

116
00:05:16,681 --> 00:05:18,801
Maybe it's something we
haven't figured out yet.

117
00:05:20,539 --> 00:05:23,259
And while orcas have left
us alone, we haven't always

118
00:05:23,259 --> 00:05:24,559
returned the favour.

119
00:05:25,359 --> 00:05:28,499
In the 1960s and 70s,
dozens of young orcas were

120
00:05:28,499 --> 00:05:30,959
captured off the Pacific
Northwest for marine parks.

121
00:05:31,699 --> 00:05:33,739
Today the Southern Resident
orcas who live there

122
00:05:33,739 --> 00:05:36,719
number only about seventy-five,
and they're endangered,

123
00:05:37,119 --> 00:05:39,199
partly because their
salmon has become scarce.

124
00:05:40,139 --> 00:05:42,539
So the next time you hear
"killer whale", remember:

125
00:05:43,079 --> 00:05:45,399
the ocean's top predator
has had every chance

126
00:05:45,399 --> 00:05:47,419
to hurt us, and it hasn't.
